References: <47ca9c0903151033jf4cd0a7ia2ec480fb2969e49@mail.gmail.com> <47ca9c0904031356m19e60954w9dbb93d683a184c1@mail.gmail.com>
Message-ID: <49D698EE.2010308@harvard.edu>




  


There are lots of ecological inference models.  Zelig includes three
_classes_of models (it doesn't yet include the one from my book,
although we're working on it).  Each one of these of course includes
many specific models.  Each one of these makes assumptions about the
process by which the individual data get aggregated into your areal
units.  If you get that process right in choosing the model, or if the
unit-level observations imply relatively narrow bounds (regardless of
whether the model is right or wrong), then you'll get good estimates
from the aggregate data.  various diagnostics may also help you choose
the right model.  Of course, information is destroyed when you
aggregate and so its no surprise that sometimes you can't recover the
individual relationships.



Gary

---

http://gking.harvard.edu





